nginx-quic-client-silent.patch  (applies to nginx 1.31.8 as bundled in OpenResty 1.31.8.1,
after nginx-quic-exiting-worker.patch and nginx-quic-send-slice.patch)

HTTP/3: a connection whose client cannot be heard any more is closed, so that the browser
moves to HTTP/2 at once instead of waiting.

Problem (seen on production on 2026-11-11, in test after test on mobile data): while an
app of the phone uploads over TCP (the Nextcloud app), nothing the browser of the same
phone sends over UDP arrives at the server. Other TCP connections of the phone pass within
a second, UDP passes only in the second in which an upload chunk ends, about every 20 s;
what the server sends still reaches the phone. Firefox keeps waiting on its HTTP/3
connection: the requests of the player hang, most are given up, and Firefox moved the
video to HTTP/2 by itself only after four minutes. There it played, during the upload.
Reproduced with Firefox ESR 153 in lab/browsers/run-blackhole.sh (UDP of
the browser's machine cut for 60 s, a 340 KB piece requested every 2 s):

    server as it was          30 s without an answer, then 15 requests fail, then HTTP/2
    server closes after 5 s   the 2 waiting requests are answered over HTTP/2 after
                              1.8 and 3.8 s, nothing fails

The reason for the 30 s: a client notices only by its own idle timeout that it is not
heard, and every packet the server sends (it repeats what is not acknowledged) starts that
timeout again.

Change: new directive

    quic_silent_timeout time;       context http, server; default 0 = off

read, like the other QUIC settings, from the default server of the listener. With a time
set, each connection is looked at when nothing has arrived from its client for half that
time:
 - the server asks (PING), and once more at three quarters of the time, so that there is
   something the client has to answer. A client with nothing in progress is asked at most
   15 times in a row without a request in between; a client that is merely idle is then
   left alone;
 - nothing has arrived for the whole time although the server asked twice: the connection
   is closed the way a reload closes it (GOAWAY and CONNECTION_CLOSE with H3_NO_ERROR).
   The browser repeats the requests that were waiting.
A client that answers notices nothing. Without the directive not one line of this runs.

Second version (2026-11-13, build name client-silent-2). The first version sent its
question only when nothing was waiting for an acknowledgement and closed only after two
probe timeouts of nginx's loss timer. That failed on production with the very phone it
was made for: a phone does not drop what the browser sends while the app uploads, it
holds it and lets it out when an upload chunk ends, up to 20 s later. Every
acknowledgement that arrives so late is a round trip sample (RFC 9002 section 5 has no
rule against it), the smoothed round trip time and with it the probe timeout grew to many
seconds, two probe timeouts no longer fitted into the 20 s between two chunk ends, and the
connection was never closed: one HTTP/3 connection lived through a whole upload, the
player's requests reached the server in bursts of 100 and more every 20 s, most of them
already given up (499), no media was delivered. The lab test of the first version dropped
the client's packets, it did not hold them, and so did not show this.
Now the questions are sent by this code at once (they do not wait for the loss timer and
ignore the congestion window, like its probes), and the time a client has to answer is
half the configured time or four times the LOWEST round trip seen, whichever is longer,
and never more than the configured time: late answers cannot raise the lowest round trip,
and where even the first answer came late the bound holds. A connection whose client is
not heard is therefore closed after 1 to 1.5 times the configured time (4 to 6 s here).
Lab (lab/silent-test.sh late: every packet of the client gets out 2.5 s
late from the handshake on, then none): first version closed after 20.2 s, this one after
the time given in README.md.

Files: ngx_event_quic.h (the setting), ngx_http_v3_module.c (the directive),
ngx_event_quic_connection.h (state), ngx_event_quic.c (timer, close),
ngx_event_quic_streams.c (a request of the client starts the count of questions again).

